        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. A receipt is 600x1500 (1:2.5). At patch size 14, how many native-resolution tokens? How many after square-resize to 336? Which loses more OCR accuracy in practice?\n\n2. Build the block-diagonal mask for a batch of four images with lengths 256, 576, 729, 1024. Verify the attention matrix is 2585x2585 and has exactly `256^2 + 576^2 + 729^2 + 1024^2` non-zero entries.\n\n3. For a 1792x896 image at patch 14, compare: (a) square-resize to 336 then encode, (b) AnyRes 2x1 + thumbnail, (c) M-RoPE at native. Which uses fewest tokens? Which preserves most detail?\n\n4. Implement fractional patch dropping: given a packed sequence, drop 50% of tokens uniformly at random, and update the block-diagonal mask accordingly. Measure the mask's sparsity change.\n\n5. Read Section 3.2 of the Qwen2-VL paper (arXiv:2409.12191). Describe in two sentences what `min_pixels` and `max_pixels` control and why both bounds matter."
      ]
    }
